hematics and Mathematical Statistics Wilberforce Road Cambridge CB30WA U.K. Gustav Holzegel","Imperial College London Department of Mathematics South Kensington Campus London SW72AZ U.K","g.holzegel@imperial.ac.uk Igor Rodnianski Princeton University"],"authors":[{"full_name":"Zerilli, F.J."}]},"raw_refs":[{"schema":"text","source":"desy","value":"[79] Zerilli, F. J., Effective potential for even-parity Regge–Wheeler gravitational perturbation equations. Phys. Rev. Lett., 24 (1970), 737–738. Mihalis Dafermos Princeton University Department of Mathematics Fine Hall, Washington Road Princeton, NJ 08544 U.S.A. dafermos@math.princeton.edu and University of Cambridge Department of Pure Mathematics and Mathematical Statistics Wilberforce Road Cambridge CB30WA U.K. Gustav Holzegel Imperial College London Department of Mathematics South Kensington Campus London SW72AZ U.K. g.holzegel@imperial.ac.uk Igor Rodnianski Princeton University"}],"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/61120"}}],"number_of_pages":146,"legacy_creation_date":"2016-01-26","preprint_date":"2016-01-24","author_count":3,"public_notes":[{"source":"arXiv","value":"146 pages, 4 figures"}],"earliest_date":"2016-01-24","refereed":true,"facet_author_name":["1044876_Mihalis Dafermos","1050881_Igor Rodnianski","1274922_Gustav H. Holzegel"],"license":[{"license":"arXiv nonexclusive-distrib 1.0","material":"preprint","url":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/"},{"imposing":"arXiv","url":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/"}],"_oai":{"sets":["Literature"],"id":"oai:inspirehep.net:1416803","updated":"2025-07-20T11:45:25.671732"},"journal_title_variants":["Acta Math.","Acta Mat."],"arxiv_eprints":[{"categories":["gr-qc","math-ph","math.AP","math.DG","math.MP"],"value":"1601.06467"}],"referenced_authors_bais":["A.Soffer.3","J.Luk.1","I.Rodnianski.1","Roy.P.Kerr.1","I.Robinson.1","A.D.Ionescu.2","E.T.Newman.1","B.F.Whiting.1","R.M.Wald.1","R.Penrose.1","R.H.Price.1","J.A.Wheeler.1","A.Trautman.1","F.Finster.1","V.Moncrief.1","W.Schlag.2","K.Martel.2","C.Gundlach.1","S.Hollands.1","T.Regge.1","B.S.Kay.1","P.Blue.2","A.A.Starobinsky.1","R.Donninger.5","C.V.Vishveshwara.1","W.Israel.1","Subrahmanyan.Chandrasekhar.1","S.Aretakis.1","S.J.Oh.1","S.Klainerman.1","F.J.Zerilli.1","S.A.Teukolsky.1","J.M.Martin.Garcia.1","O.Sarbach.1","G.Holzegel.1","P.T.Chrusciel.1","M.Tiglio.1","W.H.Press.1","Y.Shlapentokh.Rothman.1","D.Dold.1","J.Smoller.3","Martin.David.Kruskal.1","M.Dafermos.1","F.Pretorius.1","Georgios.Moschidis.1","D.B.Singleton.2","J.Keir.1","J.L.Synge.1","E.Poisson.1","Lars.Andersson.1","G.D.Dotti.1","James.M.Bardeen.1","V.Schlue.1","A.Soffer.7","J.Smulevici.2"],"legacy_version":"20200608094845.0","inspire_categories":[{"term":"Gravitation and Cosmology"},{"term":"Math and Math Physics"}],"first_author":{"full_name":"Dafermos, Mihalis","last_name":"Dafermos","first_name":"Mihalis","recid":1044876},"control_number":1416803,"dois":[{"source":"International Press","value":"10.4310/acta.2019.v222.n1.a1"},{"material":"publication","source":"arXiv","value":"10.4310/ACTA.2019.v222.n1.a1"}],"document_type":["article"],"texkeys":["Dafermos:2016uzj"],"abstracts":[{"source":"International Press","value":"We prove in this paper the linear stability of the celebrated Schwarzschild family of black holes in general relativity: Solutions to the linearisation of the Einstein vacuum equations (“linearised gravity”) around a Schwarzschild metric arising from regular initial data remain globally bounded on the black hole exterior, and in fact decay to a linearised Kerr metric. We express the equations in a suitable double null gauge. To obtain decay, one must in fact add a residual pure gauge solution which we prove to be itself quantitatively controlled from initial data. Our result a fortiori includes decay statements for general solutions of the Teukolsky equation (satisfied by gauge-invariant null-decomposed curvature components). These latter statements are in fact deduced in the course of the proof by exploiting associated quantities shown to satisfy the Regge–Wheeler equation, for which appropriate decay can be obtained easily by adapting previous work on the linear scalar wave equation. The bounds on the rate of decay to linearised Kerr are inverse polynomial, suggesting that dispersion is sufficient to control the non-linearities of the Einstein equations in a potential future proof of non-linear stability. This paper is self-contained and includes a physical-space derivation of the equations of linearised gravity around Schwarzschild from the full non-linear Einstein vacuum equations expressed in a double null gauge.","abstract_source_suggest":{"input":"International Press"}},{"source":"arXiv","value":"We prove in this paper the linear stability of the celebrated Schwarzschild family of black holes in general relativity: Solutions to the linearisation of the Einstein vacuum equations around a Schwarzschild metric arising from regular initial data remain globally bounded on the black hole exterior and in fact decay to a linearised Kerr metric. We express the equations in a suitable double null gauge. To obtain decay, one must in fact add a residual pure gauge solution which we prove to be itself quantitatively controlled from initial data. Our result a fortiori includes decay statements for general solutions of the Teukolsky equation (satisfied by gauge-invariant null-decomposed curvature components). These latter statements are in fact deduced in the course of the proof by exploiting associated quantities shown to satisfy the Regge--Wheeler equation, for which appropriate decay can be obtained easily by adapting previous work on the linear scalar wave equation. The bounds on the rate of decay to linearised Kerr are inverse polynomial, suggesting that dispersion is sufficient to control the non-linearities of the Einstein equations in a potential future proof of nonlinear stability. This paper is self-contained and includes a physical-space derivation of the equations of linearised gravity around Schwarzschild from the full non-linear Einstein vacuum equations expressed in a double null gauge.","abstract_source_suggest":{"input":"arXiv"}}],"primary_arxiv_category":["gr-qc"],"titles":[{"source":"International Press","title":"The linear stability of the Schwarzschild solution to gravitational perturbations"},{"source":"arXiv","title":"The linear stability of the Schwarzschild solution to gravitational perturbations"}],"imprints":[{"date":"2019"}],"curated":false}},{"links":{"bibtex":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/1266945?format=bibtex","latex-eu":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/1266945?format=latex-eu","latex-us":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/1266945?format=latex-us","json":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/1266945?format=json","json-expanded":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/1266945?format=json-expanded","cv":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/1266945?format=cv","citations":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?q=refersto%3Arecid%3A1266945"},"created":"2013-12-03T00:00:00+00:00","id":"1266945","updated":"2023-03-07T03:45:12.398604+00:00","metadata":{"citation_count":72,"publication_info":[{"journal_volume":"2014","artid":"023E02","year":2014,"journal_record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/journals/1211579"},"journal_title":"PTEP"}],"citation_count_without_self_citations":57,"documents":[{"filename":"scoap3-fulltext.pdf","description":"Article from SCOAP3","source":"SCOAP3","key":"9e6c1f317deefa2f8fbb2c11db46bb06","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/9e6c1f317deefa2f8fbb2c11db46bb06"}],"authors":[{"full_name_unicode_normalized":"kodama, hideo","full_name":"Kodama, Hideo","curated_relation":true,"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/authors/1002462"},"ids":[{"schema":"INSPIRE BAI","value":"Hideo.Kodama.1"}],"affiliations":[{"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/institutions/902916"},"value":"KEK, Tsukuba"},{"rec