,{"reference":{"imprint":{"publisher":"Akadémiai Kladó"},"arxiv_eprint":"gr-qc/0312012","label":"16","publication_info":{"year":2004},"title":{"title":"Proc. of the Seventh Hungarian Relativity Workshop"},"authors":[{"inspire_role":"editor","full_name":"H. Kodama, H. Ishibashi, H. Rácz, A. Kodama, A. Ishibashi, A. Rácz, I. Kodama, I. Ishibashi, I. Rácz"},{"full_name":"Kodama, H."},{"full_name":"Ishibashi, H."},{"full_name":"Rácz, H."},{"full_name":"Kodama, A."},{"full_name":"Ishibashi, A."},{"full_name":"Rácz, A."},{"full_name":"Kodama, I."},{"full_name":"Ishibashi, I."},{"full_name":"Rácz, I."}]},"raw_refs":[{"schema":"text","value":"Proc. of the Seventh Hungarian Relativity Workshop"}],"curated_relation":true,"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/634369"},"legacy_curated":true}],"number_of_pages":25,"legacy_creation_date":"2013-12-03","preprint_date":"2013-12-02","author_count":2,"public_notes":[{"source":"arXiv","value":"Minor corrections"}],"earliest_date":"2013-12-02","refereed":true,"facet_author_name":["1002462_Hideo Kodama","1059082_Ivan Arraut"],"core":true,"license":[{"imposing":"arXiv","url":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/"}],"_oai":{"sets":["Literature"],"id":"oai:inspirehep.net:1266945","updated":"2023-03-07T03:45:12.398604"},"journal_title_variants":["PTEP"],"arxiv_eprints":[{"categories":["hep-th","gr-qc","math-ph","math.MP"],"value":"1312.0370"}],"referenced_authors_bais":["Yanwen.Shang.1","Jerome.Martin.1","S.D.Odintsov.1","A.Ishibashi.1","V.Sahni.1","S.Renaux.Petel.1","Ying.Li.Zhang.1","Rachel.A.Rosen.1","E.Babichev.1","I.Arraut.1","K.Tomita.1","G.Gabadadze.1","S.Deser.1","Richard.Brito.1","A.J.Tolley.1","V.Cardoso.1","S.Nojiri.1","Andrew.K.Waldron.1","L.Berezhiani.1","Richard.P.Woodard.1","J.W.Moffat.1","H.Goto.2","A.Iglesias.1","K.Saito.165","C.de.Rham.1","J.R.Brownstein.1","P.Pani.1","G.Chkareuli.1","K.Hinterbichler.1","Hideo.Kodama.1","M.Sasaki.1","Osamu.Seto.1","M.Sandora.1","Alessandro.Fabbri.1"],"legacy_version":"20191129070000.0","inspire_categories":[{"term":"Theory-HEP"},{"term":"Gravitation and Cosmology"},{"term":"Math and Math Physics"}],"first_author":{"emails":["hideo.kodama@gmail.com"],"full_name":"Kodama, Hideo","last_name":"Kodama","first_name":"Hideo","recid":1002462},"control_number":1266945,"dois":[{"value":"10.1093/ptep/ptu016"}],"document_type":["article"],"texkeys":["Kodama:2013rea"],"abstracts":[{"source":"Oxford University Press","value":"The Schwarzschild–de Sitter solution in the Einstein theory with a positive cosmological constant $\\Lambda =m^2/\\alpha $ becomes an exact solution to the de Rham–Gabadadze–Tolley (dRGT) nonlinear massive gravity theory with the mass parameter $m$ when the theory parameters $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ satisfy the relation $\\beta =\\alpha ^2$. We study the perturbative behavior of this black hole solution in the nonlinear dRGT theory with $\\beta =\\alpha ^2$. We find that the linear perturbation equations become identical to those for the vacuum Einstein theory when they are expressed in terms of gauge-invariant variables. This implies that this black hole is stable in the dRGT theory as far as the spacetime structure is concerned, in contrast to the case of the bi-Schwarzschild solution in the bi-metric theory. However, we have also found a pathological feature that the general solution to the perturbation equations contain a single arbitrary function of spacetime coordinates. This implies a degeneracy of dynamics in the Stückelberg field sector at the linear perturbation level in this background. The physical significance of this degeneracy depends on how the Stückelberg fields couple observable fields.","abstract_source_suggest":{"input":"Oxford University Press"}},{"source":"arXiv","value":"The Schwarzschild-de Sitter solution in the Einstein theory with a positive cosmological constant $\\Lambda=m^2/\\alpha$ becomes an exact solution to the dRGT non-linear massive gravity theory with the mass parameter $m$ when the theory parameters $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ satisfy the relation $\\beta=\\alpha^2$. We study the perturbative behaviour of this black hole solution in the non-linear dRGT theory with $\\beta=\\alpha^2$. We find that the linear perturbation equations become identical to those for the vacuum Einstein theory when they are expressed in terms of the gauge-invariant variables. This implies that this black hole is stable in the dRGT theory as far as the spacetime structure is concerned in contrast to the case of the bi-Schwarzschild solution in the bi-metric theory. However, we have also found a pathological feature that the general solution to the perturbation equations contain a single arbitrary function of spacetime coordinates. This implies a degeneracy of dynamics in the St\\\"uckelberg field sector at the linear perturbation level in this background. Physical significance of this degenercy depends on how the St\\\"uckelberg fields couple observable fields.","abstract_source_suggest":{"input":"arXiv"}}],"primary_arxiv_category":["hep-th"],"titles":[{"title":"Stability of the Schwarzschild–de Sitter black hole in the dRGT massive gravity theory"},{"source":"arXiv","title":"Stability of the Schwarzschild-de Sitter black hole in the dRGT massive gravity theory"}],"imprints":[{"date":"2014-02-17"}],"curated":true}},{"links":{"bibtex":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/2630540?format=bibtex","latex-eu":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/2630540?format=latex-eu","latex-us":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/2630540?format=latex-us","json":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/2630540?format=json","json-expanded":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/2630540?format=json-expanded","cv":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/2630540?format=cv","citations":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?q=refersto%3Arecid%3A2630540"},"created":"2023-02-09T03:04:00.337155+00:00","id":"2630540","updated":"2025-07-22T07:25:28.368720+00:00","metadata":{"citation_count"